WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.200
During a time when the church&nbsp;
controlled what people could believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.920
and the kings ruled over what people ought to own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:17.760
John Locke, an English doctor, popularized three&nbsp;
ideas that changed society and parenting forever.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.760
First — people keep fighting over their beliefs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.280
because nobody can actually know which one&nbsp;
is true. To solve this problem everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.360
should have freedom of thought and the&nbsp;
right to choose their own religion.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:42.640
Second — kings can’t just do as they please,&nbsp;
because people have natural rights to liberty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.640
property, and life, and hence&nbsp;
need to be asked for permission.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.200
Third — parents should avoid&nbsp;
punishing their children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.080
since the use of emotions to make them&nbsp;
behave well can make them sensationalist.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.440
Instead, they should allow their children to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.874
guided by thoughts leading them to&nbsp;
become free-thinking rationalists.

00:01:03.874 --> 00:01:05.120
This idea laid the foundations&nbsp;
for modern education.
 &nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.000
Locke’s ideas on religion and&nbsp;
democracy became the foundation of most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.840
liberal societies, making him the father of&nbsp;
liberalism. His thoughts on education, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.240
may have been even more influential.
 &nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:25.280
Locke understood that most people doubt new ideas,&nbsp;
without any other apparent reason than them being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:33.760
uncommon. However, teaching children how to think&nbsp;
rationally and all for themselves works. Education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.840
is therefore the key to freeing society from&nbsp;
political, and psychological tyranny; and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.520
book, “Some Thoughts Concerning Education”,&nbsp;
became a parenting guide to that world.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.720
The book was originally written to help&nbsp;
his friends raise their son, Edward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:02:00.240
to become a virtuous adult — the ultimate goal of&nbsp;
education. Locke defined virtue as a combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:06.080
of self-denial and rationality — because we&nbsp;
need to control our desires to think clearly.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.080
He proposes 4 main ideas to help Edward:
 &nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.160
First, newborn babies’ minds are like&nbsp;
a blank slate - also known as tabula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.560
rasa. All knowledge comes from our perception&nbsp;
which then leaves imprints on that blank slate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.960
Because a child is highly sensitive to new&nbsp;
experiences, even the smallest association&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:38.240
of ideas can have lasting consequences.
 
If you let "a foolish maid" convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.200
Edward that ‘goblins' come out at night,&nbsp;
darkness shall forever frighten the boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.400
Locke warns. The idea that thoughts connect to&nbsp;
each other, came to be known as ‘associationism’.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.080
Second, body and mind develop together,&nbsp;
which is why Locke advises to nurture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.720
Edwards' physical needs, as much as his mental&nbsp;
ones. He recommends a plain and simple diet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.160
such as dry bread, and a small&nbsp;
non-alcoholic beer to make the boy strong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.680
Rich or spiced foods should be avoided, as they&nbsp;
are too tempting for the boy's delicate mind.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.560
To toughen the young body, abundant&nbsp;
sleep on a hard bed is ideal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:23.120
Exposing Edward to harsh conditions such as&nbsp;
cold baths protects him from the common cold. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:32.720
Third, learning should be pleasant. If children&nbsp;
find academic education exciting and can choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.840
what they like for themselves, they will&nbsp;
then apply themselves to it industriously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.680
To learn reading, Locke suggests&nbsp;
games. This self-directed approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.200
to learning allows the parents to discover&nbsp;
and nurture their child’s natural talents.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:58.160
Punishment should be avoided, as it can break a&nbsp;
child's spirit and it teaches the wrong values.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.960
To secure the best education, he urges the parents&nbsp;
to hire a tutor rather than send Edward to school.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.320
Fourth, habits always trump rules,&nbsp;
so parents should help their children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:18.160
establish clear routines and aim to&nbsp;
become role models of rational thinking.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:24.640
This will help Edward to learn how to control his&nbsp;
desires and think rationally — to become virtuous.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.880
Locke believed that good&nbsp;
habits of thought prevail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.920
while rules, which speak to the heart, fail.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.880
However, habits are important not&nbsp;
only because they always trump rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.240
but because rules consequently lead to&nbsp;
rewards and punishments - sensations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:50.080
that arouse feelings instead of reason.&nbsp;
To become a free thinker, a rationalist;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:57.040
a child shouldn't be driven by rules or incentives&nbsp;
but by esteem and fear of disgracing oneself.
 &nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:06.640
Locke's thoughts on education were published in&nbsp;
1694, and became an international bestseller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.760
Along with Rousseau's “Emile”, his book&nbsp;
became one of the most influential texts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.640
educational theory and perhaps history's&nbsp;
most important guide to parenting.
 &nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.640
John Locke once said that&nbsp;
“Reading furnishes the mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.000
only with materials of knowledge; it is&nbsp;
thinking that makes what we read ours.”

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.040
Since the same is true for videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.600
we recommend creating the habit of short&nbsp;
thinking breaks after watching something.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:42.000
Afterwards, share your thoughts with others and&nbsp;
in the same way, make new ideas forever yours.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:51.840
Try that right now and tell us&nbsp;
what you think of Locke's ideas!
